// Stashes — a rabbit that stops mid-task and picks up where it left off.
//
// `Bury` hands one value out and pauses right there. `unbury` wakes it up again. A function that
// buries anywhere in its body hands back a `stash of <what it buries>` instead of a plain value —
// nothing marks the declaration, exactly as nothing marks a function fallible and `return a
// failure` in the body decides it.
//
// A stash is not a collection. A series HAS its items; a stash PRODUCES them, one resumption at a
// time, and cannot be counted, indexed or re-read. That is what lets a stash be endless.
//
// When there is nothing left to bury, `unbury` gives back void — so the type of `unbury s` is
// `voidable T`, and asking "is it void?" is how you find out the stash is spent.

// ── Endless is fine ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Nothing here says how many. The loop never ends; it just stops running whenever it buries, and
// only runs again if somebody asks for another one.

Bind number to counting-up, given (the number first-value):
    Define next as first-value.
    Repeat:
        Bury next.
        The next becomes next + 1.
    Until false.
Done.

State "Five, from three:".
Define counter as cast counting-up on (3).
Define taken as 0.
While taken is less than 5, repeat:
    State unbury counter.
    The taken becomes taken + 1.
Done.

// ── Every stash has its own place to stand ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two casts of the same function make two stashes, and neither one knows about the other. The
// state a rabbit keeps between resumptions is its own.

State "".
State "A second counter, started later:".
Define other-counter as cast counting-up on (100).
State unbury other-counter.
State unbury counter.
State unbury other-counter.
State unbury counter.

// ── A stash that runs out ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `Skip` and `Stop` work inside a burying function the way they always do. This one walks a series,
// passes over the short words, and finishes when the series does — after which it is spent, and
// says so by handing back void.

Bind text to long-words-in, given (the series of text words):
    For each word in words, repeat:
        If the length of word is less than 4:
            Skip.
        Done.
        Bury word.
    Done.
Done.

// ── A stash is a value ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// It goes wherever a value goes: a parameter, an element of a series, a local inside another
// burying function. That last one is what lets a stash DELEGATE — hold another one and pass its
// values along — which is the difference between a generator and something you can build with.

Bind void to take-three, given (the stash of number source, the text label):
    Define taken as 0.
    While taken is less than 3, repeat:
        State label joined to ": " joined to ((unbury source but void is 0) converted to text).
        The taken becomes taken + 1.
    Done.
Done.

State "".
State "Handed to a function:".
Cast take-three on (cast counting-up on (7), "seven").

State "".
State "Three of them in a series, walked twice:".
Define many as a series of stash of number.
Insert (cast counting-up on (1)) into many.
Insert (cast counting-up on (10)) into many.
Insert (cast counting-up on (100)) into many.
For each one-stash in many, repeat:
    State unbury one-stash.
Done.
For each one-stash in many, repeat:
    State unbury one-stash.
Done.

// One stash draining another. `evens` holds a `stash of number` as an ordinary local, and that
// local has to survive `evens`' own resumptions as well as produce values of its own.
Bind number to evens-of-counting-up:
    Define inner as cast counting-up on (1).
    Repeat:
        Define value as unbury inner but void is 0.
        If value is greater than 8:
            Stop.
        Done.
        If value % 2 is 0:
            Bury value.
        Done.
    Until false.
Done.

State "".
State "One stash draining another:".
Define evens as cast evens-of-counting-up on ().
Repeat:
    Define value as unbury evens.
    If value is void:
        Stop.
    Done.
    State value.
Until false.

State "".
State "The long words, then void:".
Define found as cast long-words-in on (a series with ("a", "rabbit", "in", "the", "warren")).
Repeat:
    Define word as unbury found.
    If word is void:
        Stop.
    Done.
    State word.
Until false.
State unbury found.
State unbury found.
